export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"addon":"lighting","name":"'Lighting plots'","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"lighting-start","scope":"addon:lighting","title":"Lighting plots: getting started","summary":"What the Lighting plots add-on does in CadCraft, how to switch it on, and how to hang your first fixtures on a position.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["lighting","lighting plot","stage","theatre","fixture","instrument","position","pipe","truss","channel","dimmer","DMX","gel","unit number","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-plot","text":"Your first plot","level":2},{"id":"classes","text":"Classes","level":2},{"id":"lists-and-files","text":"Lists and files","level":2},{"id":"files-open-anywhere","text":"Files open anywhere","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELighting plots is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. You draw the positions the lights hang on (pipes, trusses, booms, floor lines), put fixtures on them, number the units along each position, give them channels and dimmer or DMX addresses, and CadCraft makes the papers a lighting job runs on: the instrument schedule, the channel hookup and a legend. It also warns you about a duplicate unit number, two fixtures on the same DMX addresses, or a dimmer with more on it than it can carry.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELighting plots\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELighting plot\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELighting plot\u003C/span\u003E, as a group at the end of the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-plot\"\u003EYour first plot\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELPOS\u003C/code\u003E. Give the position a name (\u003Ccode\u003E1E\u003C/code\u003E), choose its kind (Pipe, Truss, Boom or Floor line), click its points and press Enter.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELFIX\u003C/code\u003E, choose a fixture type, and click where each fixture goes. A fixture within the distance set in the add-on&#039;s settings of a position hangs on it. Type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E (Spread), click a position and give a number to space that many fixtures evenly along it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELNUM\u003C/code\u003E and press Enter. Unit numbers run along each position from the point you drew first, starting again at 1 on each. Type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EC\u003C/span\u003E first to give channels as well, from the number you choose.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESelect fixtures and type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELSET\u003C/code\u003E to set the purpose, colour, channel, address, wattage or a note. For the channel, address and unit, \u003Ccode\u003E101+\u003C/code\u003E numbers the selection in a row from 101, left to right (\u003Ccode\u003E101+2\u003C/code\u003E counts by two).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELSCHED\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELHOOK\u003C/code\u003E to print the schedule or the hookup, and click a point to draw it as a table in the drawing. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELLEGEND\u003C/code\u003E draws the legend.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"classes\"\u003EClasses\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFixtures are put in the class \u003Ccode\u003ELighting-Fixtures-\u003C/code\u003E plus their type (Lighting-Fixtures-Profile) and positions in \u003Ccode\u003ERigging-\u003C/code\u003E plus their kind, so \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ECLASSES\u003C/code\u003E can hide or recolour all the lights, or all the pipes, at once, and a viewport can show only one of them.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"lists-and-files\"\u003ELists and files\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EExport\u003C/span\u003E writes the instrument schedule or the channel hookup as CSV, or both with the dimmer loads as one workbook. A cell that looks like a formula is saved as text. The schedule lists each position&#039;s fixtures in unit order with a count and a wattage for the position, then the total.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-open-anywhere\"\u003EFiles open anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFixtures and positions are ordinary block references and lines in the file; with the add-on off, or in another program, they show, plot and move. The data (unit, channel, purpose and the rest) travels with each object.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Lighting plots is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. You draw the positions the lights hang on (pipes, trusses, booms, floor lines), put fixtures on them, number the units along each position, give them channels and dimmer or DMX addresses, and CadCraft makes the papers a lighting job runs on: the instrument schedule, the channel hookup and a legend. It also warns you about a duplicate unit number, two fixtures on the same DMX addresses, or a dimmer with more on it than it can carry. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › Lighting plots. Its tools are in the Lighting plot section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in Settings › CadCraft › Lighting plot, as a group at the end of the toolbar. Your first plot Type LPOS. Give the position a name (1E), choose its kind (Pipe, Truss, Boom or Floor line), click its points and press Enter. Type LFIX, choose a fixture type, and click where each fixture goes. A fixture within the distance set in the add-on's settings of a position hangs on it. Type S (Spread), click a position and give a number to space that many fixtures evenly along it. Type LNUM and press Enter. Unit numbers run along each position from the point you drew first, starting again at 1 on each. Type C first to give channels as well, from the number you choose. Select fixtures and type LSET to set the purpose, colour, channel, address, wattage or a note. For the channel, address and unit, 101+ numbers the selection in a row from 101, left to right (101+2 counts by two). Type LSCHED or LHOOK to print the schedule or the hookup, and click a point to draw it as a table in the drawing. LLEGEND draws the legend. Classes Fixtures are put in the class Lighting-Fixtures- plus their type (Lighting-Fixtures-Profile) and positions in Rigging- plus their kind, so CLASSES can hide or recolour all the lights, or all the pipes, at once, and a viewport can show only one of them. Lists and files Export writes the instrument schedule or the channel hookup as CSV, or both with the dimmer loads as one workbook. A cell that looks like a formula is saved as text. The schedule lists each position's fixtures in unit order with a count and a wattage for the position, then the total. Files open anywhere Fixtures and positions are ordinary block references and lines in the file; with the add-on off, or in another program, they show, plot and move. The data (unit, channel, purpose and the rest) travels with each object."},{"slug":"lighting-checks","scope":"addon:lighting","title":"Lighting plots: the checks","summary":"What the Lighting plots add-on warns about in Problems, and how to fix each.","section":"reference","order":20,"keywords":["problems","checks","duplicate unit","DMX overlap","dimmer overload","universe","address","footprint"],"headings":[{"id":"what-is-checked","text":"What is checked","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-is-checked\"\u003EWhat is checked\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe checks appear in the Problems panel and update when you change a fixture or a position.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA unit number used twice on a position.\u003C/strong\u003E Fix: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELNUM\u003C/code\u003E numbers them again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFixtures on no position.\u003C/strong\u003E A fixture is on a position when it lies within the distance set for the add-on of the position&#039;s line. Fix: put them on the nearest position, or move them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFixtures with no channel.\u003C/strong\u003E A note, not a warning: a plot in progress has them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETwo fixtures using the same DMX addresses.\u003C/strong\u003E A moving head or LED fixture takes several addresses (24 for a moving head, 8 for an LED wash, unless you change it). Write an address as \u003Ccode\u003E45\u003C/code\u003E (universe 1) or \u003Ccode\u003E2/045\u003C/code\u003E. Two fixtures of the same type on the same address are allowed: they are meant to follow each other.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA dimmer carrying more than its limit.\u003C/strong\u003E Fixtures with one address and one channel share a dimmer when they share an address; the limit is a setting (2400 W by default).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe wattage of a fixture is its type&#039;s usual figure until you set one: the numbers in the add-on are typical, not a maker&#039;s; use the real ones from the fixtures you hire.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What is checked The checks appear in the Problems panel and update when you change a fixture or a position. A unit number used twice on a position. Fix: LNUM numbers them again. Fixtures on no position. A fixture is on a position when it lies within the distance set for the add-on of the position's line. Fix: put them on the nearest position, or move them. Fixtures with no channel. A note, not a warning: a plot in progress has them. Two fixtures using the same DMX addresses. A moving head or LED fixture takes several addresses (24 for a moving head, 8 for an LED wash, unless you change it). Write an address as 45 (universe 1) or 2/045. Two fixtures of the same type on the same address are allowed: they are meant to follow each other. A dimmer carrying more than its limit. Fixtures with one address and one channel share a dimmer when they share an address; the limit is a setting (2400 W by default). The wattage of a fixture is its type's usual figure until you set one: the numbers in the add-on are typical, not a maker's; use the real ones from the fixtures you hire."},{"slug":"lighting-lux","scope":"addon:lighting","title":"Lighting: how much light reaches the floor","summary":"Give each fixture type its photometry file (.ies or .ldt), pick an area, and get the direct illuminance at points across it, written on the drawing.","section":"reference","order":30,"keywords":["lux","illuminance","photometry","ies","ldt","eulumdat","LPHOTO","LLUX","LUX","uniformity","direct light","floor"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELPHOTO\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELPH\u003C/code\u003E): pick a fixture type and give it a \u003Ccode\u003E.ies\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.ldt\u003C/code\u003E file from the maker. Files are kept in this browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ELLUX\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003ELUX\u003C/code\u003E): click two corners (or choose Object for a closed polyline). Type the mounting height of the fixtures (a fixture&#039;s own height wins), the height of the surface (0 = the floor), the spacing of the points, and the light loss factor.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt writes the lux at each point on layer \u003Ccode\u003ELX-LUX\u003C/code\u003E and prints the lowest, average and highest, the lowest over the average, the lumens and watts over the area and the watts per square metre.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EWhat the number is.\u003C/strong\u003E Direct light from the fixtures named, aimed straight down, at the points given: no light from walls, no shadows. It is a lower bound for a room and a good figure for a stage floor. Fixtures with no file are left out and counted. Targets (a lux level a standard asks for) are for you to type; the add-on does not carry any.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"LPHOTO (LPH): pick a fixture type and give it a .ies or .ldt file from the maker. Files are kept in this browser. LLUX (LUX): click two corners (or choose Object for a closed polyline). Type the mounting height of the fixtures (a fixture's own height wins), the height of the surface (0 = the floor), the spacing of the points, and the light loss factor. It writes the lux at each point on layer LX-LUX and prints the lowest, average and highest, the lowest over the average, the lumens and watts over the area and the watts per square metre. What the number is. Direct light from the fixtures named, aimed straight down, at the points given: no light from walls, no shadows. It is a lower bound for a room and a good figure for a stage floor. Fixtures with no file are left out and counted. Targets (a lux level a standard asks for) are for you to type; the add-on does not carry any."},{"slug":"lighting-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:lighting","title":"Lighting plots: not built yet","summary":"What the Lighting plots add-on does not do yet, so you know before you plan a job around it.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","missing","3D","light cone","gel","circuit","patch","roadmap"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA light cone in 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E Fixtures are drawn in the plan only; ModelCraft cones come later.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA maker&#039;s fixture library.\u003C/strong\u003E The fixture types are generic kinds of instrument with typical wattage and address counts. Set a fixture&#039;s own wattage and DMX footprint as you need them.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECircuit and patch sheets.\u003C/strong\u003E The hookup lists channel, address and load; a patch of dimmers into circuits and racks is not made.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EGel and gobo schedules.\u003C/strong\u003E The colour is a text you type; it is listed but not counted by stock.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESection views.\u003C/strong\u003E Trim heights are kept on a position but a section through the rig is not drawn.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet A light cone in 3D. Fixtures are drawn in the plan only; ModelCraft cones come later. A maker's fixture library. The fixture types are generic kinds of instrument with typical wattage and address counts. Set a fixture's own wattage and DMX footprint as you need them. Circuit and patch sheets. The hookup lists channel, address and load; a patch of dimmers into circuits and racks is not made. Gel and gobo schedules. The colour is a text you type; it is listed but not counted by stock. Section views. Trim heights are kept on a position but a section through the rig is not drawn."}]};
